\name{The SCSMR model}
\alias{scsmr}
\title{
Simplicial constrained spatial median regressio (SCSMR) for compositional responses and predictors
}

\description{
Simplicial constrained spatial median regressio (SCSMR) for compositional responses and predictors.
}

\usage{
scsmr(y, x, xnew = NULL, tol = 1e-06, eps = 1e-6)
}

\arguments{
\item{y}{
A matrix with the compositional data (dependent variable). Zero values are allowed.
}
\item{x}{
A matrix with the compositional predictors. Zero values are allowed.
}
\item{xnew}{
If you have new data use it, otherwise leave it NULL.
}
\item{tol}{
The tolerance value to terminate the IRLS algortihm.
}
\item{eps}{
A small value to prevent or safeguard agains no solution of the quadratic programming.
}
}

\details{
The function performs spatial median regression where the beta coefficients are constained
to be positive and sum to 1. The inspiration came from the SCLS model of Tsagris (2025).
The function uses quadratic programming (package quadprog) and the solution is very fast.
}

\value{
A list including:
\item{norm}{
The mean of the square root of the sum of squares.
}
\item{be}{
The beta coefficients.
}
\item{est}{
The fitted of xnew if xnew is not NULL.
}
}

\references{
Tsagris. M. (2025). Constrained least squares simplicial-simplicial regression.
Statistics and Computing, 35(27).
}

\author{
Michail Tsagris.

R implementation and documentation: Michail Tsagris \email{mtsagris@uoc.gr}.
}

